Now let me understand the vulnerability better. The crash is a **stack-buffer-overflow READ** in `is_http`. Let me look at the `is_http` function again and think about how the overflow occurs.

In `harness_channel_data`:
```c
uint8_t buf[8192] = {0};
memcpy(buf, Data, Size);
...
int mlen_tcp = stun_get_message_len_str(buf, Size, 1, &app_len_tcp);
```

The buffer is 8192 bytes, zero-initialized. `Size` can be up to 8192. So `buf` is null-terminated at position `Size` (since it was zero-initialized).

In `is_http`:
```c
int is_http(const char *s, size_t blen) {
  if (s && blen >= 12) {
    if ((strstr(s, "GET ") == s) || (strstr(s, "POST ") == s) || ...
```

`strstr(s, "GET ")` scans `s` for "GET ". Since `s` is null-terminated at position `Size`, `strstr` won't read past the buffer.

But wait - let me look at the `findstr` function more carefully:

```c
static inline const char *findstr(const char *hay, size_t slen, const char *needle, bool ignore_case) {
  const char *ret = NULL;

  if (hay && slen && needle) {
    const size_t nlen = strlen(needle);
    if (nlen <= slen) {
      const size_t smax = slen - nlen + 1;
      const char *sp = hay;
      for (size_t i = 0; i < smax; ++i) {
        if (sheadof(needle, sp + i, ignore_case)) {
          ret = sp + i;
          break;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return ret;
}
```

And `sheadof`:
```c
static inline bool sheadof(const char *head, const char *full, bool ignore_case) {
  while (*head) {
    if (*head != *full) {
      if (ignore_case && (tolower((int)*head) == tolower((int)*full))) {
        // OK
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    }
    ++head;
    ++full;
  }
  return true;
}
```

The `sheadof` function reads `*full` while `*head` is non-zero. The needle " HTTP/" has length 6. So `sheadof` reads exactly 6 bytes from `full`. In `findstr`, `sp + i` ranges from `hay` to `hay + slen - nlen`. So `sheadof` reads from `hay + slen - nlen` to `hay + slen - nlen + nlen = hay + slen`. That's within bounds.

Hmm, but wait. Let me look at the `is_http` function again more carefully:

```c
int is_http(const char *s, size_t blen) {
  if (s && blen >= 12) {
    if ((strstr(s, "GET ") == s) || (strstr(s, "POST ") == s) || (strstr(s, "DELETE ") == s) ||
        (strstr(s, "PUT ") == s)) {
      const char *sp = findstr(s + 4, blen - 4, " HTTP/", false);
      if (sp) {
        sp += 6;
        const size_t diff_blen = sp - s;
        if (diff_blen + 4 <= blen) {
          sp = findstr(sp, blen - diff_blen, "\r\n\r\n", false);
          if (sp) {
            int ret_len = (int)(sp - s + 4);
            const char *clheader = "content-length: ";
            const char *cl = findstr(s, sp - s, clheader, true);
            if (cl) {
              const unsigned long clen = strtoul(cl + strlen(clheader), NULL, 10);
              if (clen > 0 && clen < (0x0FFFFFFF)) {
                ret_len += (int)clen;
              }
            }
            return ret_len;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return 0;
}
```

The key issue: `strstr(s, "GET ")` etc. are called on `s` which is the buffer. In `harness_channel_data`, the buffer is `uint8_t buf[8192] = {0}` and `Size` bytes are copied. So `buf[Size]` is 0 (null terminator). `strstr` will stop at the null terminator.

But wait - what about `harness_stun_client`? It uses `stun_buffer buf;` which is a stack variable. Let me check if `buf.buf` is zero-initialized:

```c
stun_buffer buf;
buf.len = Size;
memcpy(buf.buf, Data, buf.len);
```

`stun_buffer buf;` is NOT zero-initialized! It's a stack variable. So `buf.buf` contains uninitialized data beyond `Size` bytes. But `stun_is_command_message(&buf)` is called first, and if it returns false, the function returns early.

